Below is a minimal **JUnit 5** helper that calls the supplied native‑code wrapper (`MethodInvokeTest.checkInjectedInvoker`) and asserts that the *caller‑sensitive* method inserted the correct “invoker” class onto the stack.

```java
package com.mycompany.tests;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertTrue;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

// Import the generated binding – adjust package/name according to your build setup.
// If the generator put it under `org.mydomain.functions`, change the import below:
import org.mydomain.functions.MethodInvokeTest;

/**
 * Unit‑test that verifies the caller‑sensitive logic inside {@link CSM}.
 *
 * <p>In our case we want to make sure that when {@code csmInstance.someCallerSensitive()}
 * runs, the JVM pushes an invoker whose declaring class equals
 * {@code MyExpectedClass}.  The helper method performs all the heavy lifting
 * (stack inspection + comparison).  It simply reports back whether the
 * expectation was met.</p>
 */
public class CallerSensitiveValidationTest {

    /**
     * Example CSM instance used only for illustration purposes.
     * Replace this with whatever concrete implementation you actually have.
     */
    private final CSM csmInstance = new ConcreteCsm();   // <-- replace!

    @Test
    void verifyCorrectInvokerIsPresentOnStack() {
        /*
         * NOTE ABOUT THE PARAMETERS
         * ------------------------
         * - The first argument (`csm`) is passed as a plain Java object –
         *   the underlying JNI layer will receive its real runtime value.
         * - The second argument (`expected`) is the fully–qualified name of
         *   the class that should appear on top of the stack after the
         *   caller‑sensitive method executes.
         *
         * Because the helper accepts arbitrary Java objects represented as
         * strings, we simply forward the two values without converting them
         * to JSON or anything fancy.  The native side knows how to interpret
         * them.
         */

        boolean matches =
                MethodInvokeTest.checkInjectedInvoker(
                        /* csm */      csmInstance,
                        /* expected */ "com.mycompany.MyExpectedClass"
                );

        // Assert that the condition holds – otherwise the test fails.
        assertTrue(matches,
                   () -> "Expected invoker class "
                         + "\"com.mycompany.MyExpectedClass\" not found on stack");
    }
}
```

### How it works

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| 1️⃣ | Your test creates / obtains a live `CSM` instance (`csmInstance`). |
| 2️⃣ | You invoke the helper `<native>` method:<br/>`checkInjectedInvoker(csmInstance, "fully.Qualified.Expected")`. |
| 3️⃣ | Inside the native implementation the following checks occur:<ul><li>The current thread’s stack trace is inspected.<li>An entry corresponding to the *injected invoker* is located (the one added by the caller‑sensitive machinery).</li><li>Its declaring class is compared to the supplied `expected` string.</li></ul> |
| 4️⃣ | A Boolean return value indicates success/failure. |

If the assertion passes, you’re guaranteed that the caller‑sensitive method behaved exactly as intended.  
If it fails, either the wrong class was pushed or no invoker at all appeared—both situations warrant investigation.

---

#### Quick sanity checklist

| ✅ | Item |
|---|------|
| ✔️ | Make sure the `csmInstance` really triggers the caller‑sensitive path during the test run. |
| ✔️ | Verify that `"com.mycompany.MyExpectedClass"` is spelled exactly as the compiled class’ canonical name. |
| ✔️ | Ensure the native bridge (`MethodInvokeTest`) is properly linked (JNI shared lib present, proper package/class naming). |
| ✔️ | Run the test with sufficient verbosity so you see the failure reason if it occurs. |

That’s all there is to it! Happy testing.

